{
  "preference": "A",
  "reason": "A better supports the director’s decision: its Design assessment says amnesty “may explain” the shared improvement, whereas B’s Causal assessment asserts without evidence that amnesty “affected both groups equally.” A also avoids B’s unsupported justification for a 10-point adoption threshold. Both correctly identify the unchanged 20-point gap and propose randomized offers, but A’s Proposed next test incorrectly says staff “did randomize assignment,” and both decision rules ignore statistical uncertainty.",
  "reports": {
    "A": {
      "task_fidelity": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Addresses the requested comparisons, causal assessment, feasible next test and advance decision criterion with source citations. The proposed experiment is partly misrepresented as already completed.",
        "excerpt": "Proposed next test: “Because staff did randomize assignment, an intention-to-treat comparison ... would support a causal claim.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2: “Reminder enrollment was voluntary, not randomized”; staff “can randomize an offer of reminders next month.”"
      },
      "facts_and_evidence": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "All numerical comparisons are correct, including the equivalent of a zero-point difference-in-differences. However, the assertion of completed randomization contradicts the sources.",
        "excerpt": "Descriptive results: “The gap between groups was 20 points in both the prior month and the pilot month”; Proposed next test: “staff did randomize assignment.”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 gives prior counts of 75 and 55 and pilot counts of 80 and 60, each out of 100. S2 says enrollment was not randomized."
      },
      "explanation_and_synthesis": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Clearly connects baseline imbalance, equal changes and voluntary enrollment to the absence of demonstrated causality. It could more explicitly distinguish the effect of offering reminders from the effect of receiving them.",
        "excerpt": "Design assessment: “The 20-point group gap is descriptive, not causal evidence.” Proposed next test: “an intention-to-treat comparison ... would support a causal claim.”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 establishes the unchanged baseline gap. S2 distinguishes randomized offer assignment from uptake by requiring both to be recorded."
      },
      "counterevidence_and_boundaries": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Appropriately treats selection and amnesty as possible explanations rather than established causes, and notes missing trends. The adoption rule nevertheless omits uncertainty and treats a below-threshold estimate as sufficient reason to stop.",
        "excerpt": "Design assessment: “A fine-amnesty campaign ... may explain the common 5-point rise.” Decision criterion: “otherwise, stop.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 says amnesty reached both groups and earlier trends and opt-in reasons were not recorded; neither source establishes amnesty’s effect or supplies precision estimates."
      },
      "reader_usefulness": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Provides a practical randomized-offer design and correctly recommends intention-to-treat analysis. The clearly labeled 5-point recommendation is permissible, but management should justify the threshold and specify how uncertainty affects adoption.",
        "excerpt": "Decision criterion: “management must define the smallest worthwhile benefit — I recommend 5 percentage points.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 permits randomized offers and requires management to set the smallest worthwhile benefit before examining results; it supplies no particular threshold."
      }
    },
    "B": {
      "task_fidelity": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Covers the requested comparisons, design critique, next test and decision criterion using source citations. Its causal overstatements and unsupported threshold rationale weaken fulfillment of the central decision task.",
        "excerpt": "Proposed next test: “I propose a 10-point difference ... since the pilot suggests plausible confounding effects of this size.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 requires an advance management-defined threshold but provides no estimate of a worthwhile benefit or confounding-effect magnitude."
      },
      "facts_and_evidence": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "The percentages and changes are accurate, but equal exposure is incorrectly converted into equal causal effects, and the claimed 10-point confounding magnitude has no source support.",
        "excerpt": "Causal assessment: “the amnesty affected both groups equally.” Proposed next test: “confounding effects of this size.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 states only that amnesty “reached both groups.” S1 shows equal 5-point changes and a persistent 20-point gap, neither of which identifies a 10-point confounding effect."
      },
      "explanation_and_synthesis": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "Recognizes the crucial baseline comparison but overinterprets it. An unchanged observed gap does not establish that reminders had no effect, and a confounding magnitude would not itself establish a worthwhile benefit.",
        "excerpt": "Causal assessment: “it reflects pre-existing differences ... not the reminders' effect”; “reminders did not widen the existing gap.”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 supports an unchanged observed gap. S2’s voluntary enrollment and missing earlier trends prevent identifying the counterfactual gap without reminders."
      },
      "counterevidence_and_boundaries": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "Lists important design limitations but undermines that caution with unsupported causal certainty about amnesty and reminders. It also omits uncertainty from the adoption rule.",
        "excerpt": "Causal assessment: “With voluntary enrollment, no prior-trend data, and no recorded opt-in reasons ... the pilot design cannot support a causal claim”; “the amnesty affected both groups equally.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 confirms the listed limitations, but does not establish equal amnesty effects. S1’s equal changes cannot resolve those limitations."
      },
      "reader_usefulness": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "The randomized-offer proposal is feasible, but the unsupported rationale for a demanding 10-point threshold could materially misdirect adoption. The binary rule also provides no treatment of inconclusive results.",
        "excerpt": "Decision criterion: “Adopt reminders only if the randomized comparison shows at least the preset difference; otherwise decline.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 supports randomizing offers and recording assignment, uptake and returns, but leaves the worthwhile-benefit threshold to management; neither source supports the proposed 10-point rationale."
      }
    }
  },
  "condition_clues": "None reliably identifying a condition observed. Both use similar section headings and source labels; these were not treated as evidence of quality or provenance."
}
